well i called the council the world  athletics council together today to  discuss the situation and to cut to the  chase  we have now excluded  our  authorized neutral athletes they're  russian athletes they've been given that  status  uh for uh many years and for obvious  reasons around anti-doping  and also the belarusian athletes we've  excluded them from our world  championship series this year  and for the foreseeable future so we  have five world championships in  different guises we have a in fact i'm  sitting talking to in oman on friday we  have the world race walk  championships  and so we had to move quickly to make  sure that we were able to convey  our  well just the appalling nature of what  we witnessed and  the issue for us now is that those  athletes will not be competing here or  in any of our world championships this  year  and this is a complete ban they're not  allowed to compete you know under a  neutral flag or with no national anthem  no russian flag a complete ban  yeah they will not be competing in any  of our world championship events and we  have five world championships this year  on four different continents but be very  clear about it um they will not be the  russian athletes will not be competing  under uh neutral status which they have  and the belarusian athletes will not be  competing either they are excluded  now i know and we've talked many times  about this that you are instinctively i  think against  um boycotts and sporting boycotts  because you think it  um you know hurts the athletes i mean  you competed in 1980 for instance what  is different about this what makes this  uh something that you think um deserves  an all-out ban  well mark first of all you're right it's  not my natural instinct i didn't come  into the sport and i certainly didn't  seek to become president of the sport to  sanction athletes and prevent them from  competing but  this is a very very different scale of  problem you know the russian athletes  have been banned in our sport and we've  been very tough with the only member  federation that chose to suspend the  russian federation because of it  egregious doping  uh an egregious doping record and  incursion into our own integrity  but look i've always fought hard for  maintaining the primacy of the athletes  and the independence of sport  but on this occasion i think we have to  recognize there is a global uh response  it's not that sport is  alone as it has in the past carried the  burden when other organizations  governments it's been business as usual  we have to recognize that we're part of  something far bigger and our own  interests at the moment have to be  secondary to  the  to you know the the united nature of  solidarity here uh it's a modest  contribution but we were very very clear  the council was very clear uh that this  was on a scale i mean the  appalling acts of barbarity that are  being witnessed every minute uh in under  you know in under this situation is  something that my sport and i know many  many other sports uh  can't just sit the dance out not on this  occasion  and i um heard someone trying to defend  you know continuing with russian  athletes and belarusian athletes  the other day but  as someone pointed out here to me  yesterday what about ukrainian athletes  these ukrainian athletes can't do  anything at the moment and a lot of the  ukrainian athletes are actually signing  up for a volunteer force to fight uh  president putin's army um have you been  horrified what's what's been happening  here  yes i have and mark you are you know  you're in the epicenter of it  um but i i none of us could sit here and  accept  uh russian athletes or belarusian  athletes competing while we have  ukrainian athletes some in bunkers some  not able to leave the country um it just  doesn't it felt entirely wrong and look  just a few hours literally an hour and a  half before my council meeting this  afternoon that uh sanctioned that  excluded those athletes i was you know  in a very interrupted facetime  conversation with a very senior figure  in ukrainian athletics who was you know  in a bunker protecting his family this  is unsustainable and we had to do  something and you know we've been as  tough as we possibly can and i think  other sports are  following uh in exactly the same way and  this has to be a global response this  can't just be borne by governments or  you know the united nations everybody  that is in a position to make their  feelings truly felt has has to be  articulate and clear about it  yeah and what about fifa and the ioc i  mean two organizations that  if i can say it put it this way not  quick to find their moral conscience  well look let's not go back into the  history of this sport has responded i  think in the right way  we were very clear  five years ago that the level of doping  that was taking place in russia was  unacceptable it was destroying the  fabric of our sport  we suspended the russian federation  we have a road map for reinstatement  that clearly is now seriously in  jeopardy  and we need to make sure that uh you  know the purpose and the values of our  sport shine through it's not always easy  sometimes decisions have to be made that  are unnecessarily pragmatic and you want  to always defend  the primacy and the independence of  sport but this goes way way beyond any  philosophical discussions  about athletes or even the the right of  a federation to exist  this is now uh so serious that sport has  to  not sit the dance it cannot sit the  dance out on this okay
 

